<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Olmsted returns with his caregiver three months after his first visit, nine weeks after starting donepezil 5 mg each morning. He has had no nausea, diarrhea, loss of appetite, dizziness, fainting, or vivid dreams. His caregiver feels he is a little more engaged in the mornings, and his memory is otherwise unchanged. The night-time confusion during his cold three weeks ago stopped as soon as the diphenhydramine cold medicine was stopped, and his cough has gone. He stopped driving after the consultation and walks with company most days. He wears a medical identification bracelet. His caregiver completed the Zarit Burden Interview today and describes broken sleep, worry about leaving him alone, and little time off. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medicine</td><td>Donepezil 5 mg for nine weeks; no side effects</td></tr><tr><td>Course</td><td>Memory unchanged; slightly more engaged in the mornings</td></tr><tr><td>Interval illness</td><td>Cold with night-time confusion from diphenhydramine three weeks ago, resolved</td></tr><tr><td>Safety</td><td>No driving; medical identification bracelet; no wandering</td></tr><tr><td>Caregiver</td><td>Zarit Burden Interview 24; broken sleep and little time off</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

