    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Mild dementia, type undetermined (F03.A0)</content>. Donepezil 5 mg tolerated; heart rate 62 with no fainting, and weight down 0.6 kg in two months. No new behavioral symptoms. Caregiver burden is moderate (Zarit 24, above the threshold of 21). [S]<list><item>Increase donepezil to 10 mg each morning; report fainting, slow pulse, vomiting, diarrhea, or weight loss.</item><item>Caregiver: education program and a trial of an adult day program two days a week; Alzheimer&apos;s Association respite information.</item><item>Advance care planning completed today.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medication review with the AGS Beers Criteria (anticholinergic burden, sedatives, sleeping pills, antipsychotics)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Diphenhydramine cold medicine stopped after the sick visit; no anticholinergic or sedating drugs now</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Neuropsychiatric symptom screen (NPI-Q)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Mild apathy, less in the mornings; no hallucinations, agitation, or wandering; the night-time confusion has not recurred</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Cholinesterase inhibitor tolerability and benefit: heart rate, fainting, nausea, diarrhea, weight, sleep and dreams</td><td>Each follow-up on treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>On 5 mg for nine weeks: heart rate 62, no fainting, no gastrointestinal effects, weight 71.2 kg (down 0.6 kg), no vivid dreams; increasing to 10 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antipsychotic appropriateness and taper review</td><td>Every visit if on an antipsychotic</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not on an antipsychotic</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Vascular risk-factor control (blood pressure, lipids, glucose, smoking)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Blood pressure 128/76 mmHg; lipids at the wellness visit</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Home safety evaluation</td><td>At diagnosis and each follow-up</td><td>Deferred</td><td>The home safety checklist given at diagnosis was not reviewed at this visit, a deviation from the each-follow-up item. There have been no falls, burns, or wandering, so no harm is evident. Corrective action: the checklist is reviewed at the six-month visit</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Cognitive test (MoCA)</td><td>Annually; every visit if a change is reported</td><td>Up to date</td><td>MoCA 19 three months ago; no change reported</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>ADL (Katz) and IADL (Lawton-Brody)</td><td>Annually, and at any reported change</td><td>Up to date</td><td>No change reported since the first visit</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Dementia stage (CDR or FAST)</td><td>Annually</td><td>Up to date</td><td>CDR 1 two months ago</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>Annually, and at any reported change</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 6 three months ago; mood steadier today</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Fall-risk assessment (Timed Up and Go, gait and balance)</td><td>Annually</td><td>Up to date</td><td>No falls; Timed Up and Go 11 seconds three months ago</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Driving safety (CDR-based)</td><td>At diagnosis and annually</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No driving since the consultation</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Firearms, wandering, and financial safety</td><td>At diagnosis and with any change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No firearms; medical identification bracelet worn; no wandering; caregiver oversees finances</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Caregiver burden (Zarit Burden Interview) and caregiver needs</td><td>At diagnosis, then every 6 to 12 months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Zarit Burden Interview 24, moderate burden (21 or more): education, adult day program, and respite information given; the item missed at diagnosis is now done</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Advance care planning and goals of care</td><td>At diagnosis, then annually and at stage changes</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Health care agent named and his values and wishes recorded today, with him taking full part</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>TSH, vitamin B12, blood count, and metabolic panel</td><td>At diagnosis; repeat only with acute cognitive worsening</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal at diagnosis; no acute change to repeat them</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Syphilis and HIV testing</td><td>Only with risk factors or atypical features</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No risk factors</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Structural brain MRI, non-contrast</td><td>Once at diagnosis; repeat only with an atypical course or focal change</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Done once at diagnosis; no atypical course</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Amyloid or tau biomarkers (blood, CSF, or amyloid PET) and APOE genotype</td><td>Before anti-amyloid therapy, or when the result would change care</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Declined two months ago; no change in his wishes</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Anti-amyloid antibody therapy with ARIA surveillance MRI</td><td>Only if anti-amyloid therapy is started</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not chosen</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Lifestyle: aerobic exercise about twice a week, hearing, vision, sleep, social engagement</td><td>Ongoing</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Walks most days with company; adult day program will add social contact</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Dementia care navigation and caregiver support program</td><td>At enrollment, with ongoing support</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Caregiver education program and adult day program arranged</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Vaccination status (influenza, pneumococcal, zoster, COVID-19, Tdap)</td><td>Yearly, per the CDC schedule</td><td>Due</td><td>At the wellness visit in about six weeks</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Complete the reversible-cause tests and brain MRI and discuss the diagnosis with him and his caregiver by the four-week visit. Outcome by 1895-06-02: Tests and MRI showed no reversible cause; diagnosis of mild dementia of undetermined type discussed with both [S]</item><item>A safety plan in place by the three-month review: no driving, medicines supervised, finances overseen, and medical identification worn. Outcome by 1895-08-04: Stopped driving after the consultation; caregiver gives medicines from a weekly organizer and oversees bill paying with his agreement; medical identification bracelet worn [S]</item><item>Donepezil at 10 mg daily without side effects and with resting heart rate 55 or above by the six-month review. In progress; target 1895-10-27 [S]</item><item>Caregiver burden assessed and support in place by the six-month review. In progress; target 1895-10-27 [S]</item><item>Name a health care agent and record his values and wishes while he can take full part, by the three-month review. Outcome by 1895-08-04: Health care agent named and his wishes recorded at the three-month review [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Practice parameter: diagnosis of dementia (an evidence-based review). Report of the Quality Standards Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology (2001); Practice parameter update: evaluation and management of driving risk in dementia: report of the Quality Standards Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology (2010); Alzheimer&apos;s Association clinical practice guideline for the Diagnostic Evaluation, Testing, Counseling, and Disclosure of Suspected Alzheimer&apos;s Disease and Related Disorders (DETeCD-ADRD): Executive summary of recommendations for primary care (2025); American Geriatrics Society 2023 updated AGS Beers Criteria for potentially inappropriate medication use in older adults (2023); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, Non-Alzheimer&apos;s dementia and Alzheimer&apos;s disease monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Annual wellness visit in about six weeks. Six-month dementia review in about three months. Next scheduled visit 1895-09-15: subsequent annual wellness visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
